NAME
    cpp - C source preprocessor (//#use splicer)

SYNOPSIS
    cpp src.c >out.c
    cpp src.c | ...

DESCRIPTION
    Expands the `//#use NAME` directives in a C source and writes the
    combined source to stdout. Each directive is replaced with the
    contents of lib_NAME.c from the same directory, recursively (a library
    may //#use others) and deduplicated, so shared helpers are pulled in
    by concatenation (p8cc has no #include or linker). A source with no
    //#use passes through unchanged.

    cpp is pass 1 of the on-target C toolchain — the native counterpart of
    the host tools/clib.py. Its output feeds the compiler:
        cpp foo.c >foo.i
        (then the compiler on foo.i)

    Because the BIOS has a single read stream, cpp emits every library
    ahead of the source rather than at each directive's exact position;
    the result compiles identically (p8cc lays functions out by symbol
    table, and definitions still precede uses).

NOTES
    Libraries are looked up as lib_NAME.c in the source's directory.
    P8XFS filenames are at most 12 characters, so libraries whose file
    name exceeds that (lib_abspath.c, lib_readline.c) cannot yet be stored
    on the card for on-target preprocessing.

EXAMPLES
    cpp cat.c >cat.i        splice glob/globx/dirent into cat.c
    cpp pwd.c >pwd.i        a source with no //#use: passes through

